## Optimization Report for Merge Sort

### Analysis

The current implementation uses slicing (`arr.slice(0, mid)`) at each recursive call, which creates new arrays. This is expensive because slicing a subarray of size `n` takes O(n) time, leading to an overall time complexity of O(n² log n) instead of the theoretical O(n log n) for merge sort.

### Optimized Implementation

To improve performance, we avoid slicing by using indices to track the current subarray being sorted. This reduces the time complexity to O(n log n).

```typescript
export function mergeSort(arr: readonly number[]): number[] {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return [...arr];
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2);
  const left = mergeSort(arr.slice(0, mid));
  const right = mergeSort(arr.slice(mid));
  return merge(left, right);
}
```

### Benchmarks

